WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.002 --> 00:00:02.460
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.060
I help some streaming chefs
avoid performance heartburn.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.922
Will we master the
secret ingredient?

00:00:07.922 --> 00:00:08.880
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.870
Previously we covered
the various ways

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.090
to profile and get
visibility into the startup

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.910
time for Kubernetes pod, which
is important since that's

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.470
exactly where the Bacon, Live!

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:20.961
Team was running into a problem.

00:00:20.961 --> 00:00:23.460
Their startup performance was
behaving like a piece of dried

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:24.890
out kale--

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.120
because it's a food app--

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.490
it's a pun.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.000
Anyhow, let's gets to profiling.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.530
Bacon, Live! had
ported their code

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.410
from Compute Engine to
Container Engine, and as such,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.000
all of their
containers initialized

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.390
in the parallel phase
of the pod boot process.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.090
In fairness, they did have
a single Init Container that

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.010
was doing some,
"sorry, bald guy,

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.810
we can't tell you
about that work,"

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.880
but we'll ignore that for now.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.950
Applying the profiling
process from before,

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.117
we ended up getting
the following data.

00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:54.450
Three of the containers were
taking a significant amount

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:55.530
of time to boot up.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.450
Most impacted were the
public and admin endpoints,

00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.850
which was problematic
since those

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.400
were crucial for the
usage of their system.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.379
Looking at that code
for the public endpoint,

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.850
the reason for this problem
became crystal clear.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:08.850
The initialization code
for the public container

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.334
was doing a quick call
over to the stats container

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:12.750
to make sure it
was up and running

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.340
before proceeding forward.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.380
The problem is
that the URL fetch

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.447
command is a blocking call.

00:01:18.447 --> 00:01:20.280
The result is that the
public endpoints Init

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.860
code was waiting around
for the stats container

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.260
to finish initializing
before moving forward.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.494
Obviously, this was not ideal.

00:01:27.494 --> 00:01:29.160
Since it looked like
we had a dependency

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.050
on the stats container,
our first attempt

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.020
to solve this involved moving
that container into the Init

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.510
phase so that the parallel
containers didn't have to get

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.310
caught in this
weird race condition

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.050
and could execute properly.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.170
But when we timed things
again, we realized our mistake.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.270
The Init phase got two
to four seconds longer,

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.270
which resulted in the
entire system startup

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:49.326
being worse off in
general since all

00:01:49.326 --> 00:01:51.450
of the rest of the containers
were delayed as well.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.910
This was obviously
the wrong direction

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.859
and made the whole
boot time slower.

00:01:55.859 --> 00:01:57.150
At this point, the Bacon, Live!

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.850
Team decided to go back through
and make some small changes

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:00.900
to their code.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.870
The Stats module was there
to help do timings of startup

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.750
related tasks, but since they
now had visibility into those

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:10.020
things outside of their code--
thanks to the profiling harness

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:10.971
we'd set up for them--

00:02:10.971 --> 00:02:12.720
they were happy to
remove the dependencies

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:13.939
for these endpoints.

00:02:13.939 --> 00:02:16.230
This allowed them to get the
same timing information as

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.090
before and move
the stats container

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.757
back to the parallel phase
without the dependencies.

00:02:20.757 --> 00:02:22.590
The result was about
two seconds improvement

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.350
in availability time for
their public endpoints.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:26.490
The Bacon, Live!

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.310
Team realized a critical
pattern for startup performance

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.520
in Kubernetes-- avoid
parallel Init dependencies

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:33.759
where possible.

00:02:33.759 --> 00:02:35.550
Thankfully, we were
able to profile it, get

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.550
visibility of the problem, and
fix it before anybody noticed.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.830
If you'd like to learn more
tips and tricks for making

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.422
your cloud as fast
as possible, make

00:02:42.422 --> 00:02:44.880
sure to check out the rest of
the "Cloud Performance Atlas"

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.010
content because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.390
every millisecond counts.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:51.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

